TI  - Where is all the NIH data? A Landscape Analysis of the 150 NIH Repositories and
      the Path Beyond the Mountains.
LID - 10.2139/ssrn.5374625 [doi]
AB  - The National Institutes of Health (NIH) is heavily invested in developing data
      repositories. To date, the NIH has funded hundreds of separate repositories,
      hosting exabytes of diverse data types that enable scientific research across
      domains, organisms and diseases. These data drive scientific discovery and power
      the secondary data use economy guided by principles of findability, accessibility,
      interoperability and reusability (FAIR). While this explosion in data resources
      should in theory make more data available for research, it also scales new
      operational challenges. Looking beyond the FAIRness of the data hosted in the
      repositories, we must examine the FAIRness of the repositories themselves. In this
      paper, we report findings from landscape analysis of NIH repositories. Using a
      structured search approach, we inventoried 143 active NIH repositories for the
      types of data they contain, characterize the access mechanisms unique to each
      repository, and estimate their operational costs based on publicly available
      information on NIH Reporter. A total of 46 repositories (32.2%) hosted human
      genomic data, 14 repositories (9.8%) held non-human genomic data, 33 repositories
      (23.1%) included imaging data, and 38 repositories (26.6%) hosted clinical data.
      Between 1989 and 2024, the NIH has allocated an estimated $6,718,994,379.00 for
      repository development, with the National Cancer Institute as the largest
      single-institute funder. We instead propose that funders should prioritize funding
      for fewer, better connected data repositories that serve distinct purposes.
